{
  "dom_id": "step-q153986",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $9\\ge -\\frac{x}{4}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The variable $x$ is being *divided* by $4.$ To isolate $x$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-4$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ (-4)\\cdot 9\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,(-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ -36\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,(-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-36\\,\\, & \\le (-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ -36 & \\le (\\cancel{-}4)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ -36 & \\le 4\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel the common factor $4$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-36 & \\le 4\\cdot \\frac{x}{4} \\\\ -36 & \\le \\frac{4x}{4} \\\\ -36 & \\le \\frac{\\cancel{4}x}{\\cancel{4}} \\\\ -36 & \\le x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the inequality with the variable on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx\\ge -36\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -36.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
